---
/**
 * Venture story · American Film Market 2025.
 *
 * ARCHIVE-REWORK 2026-08-03. The previous build told 3 of the operator's 9
 * AFM dispatches (kickoff/Waymo, the Pitch Conference, the FLIP indie-action
 * panel) and left the other 6 — including the two sessions where he asked a
 * question from the floor, and his two highest-reach posts of the week —
 * entirely off the page. Meanwhile 12 frames were baked into
 * public/media/venture/afm-2025 and never rendered. This pass rebuilds the
 * page as the WEEK it actually was: seven movements, Monday arrival through
 * Saturday's closing panel, each panel photo sitting with the dispatch it
 * belongs to. Source posts (all his own, E:\_LINKEDIN-ARCHIVE\afm-2025):
 *   2025-11-11 afm-filmmaking ............ arrival / Waymo / drone connections
 *   2025-11-12 afm-general ............... Finance I: From Packaging to Payback
 *   2025-11-12 afm-filmfinance ........... The Innovators: From Fan to Financier
 *   2025-11-12 afm-filmmaking ............ distribution conversations, Waymo
 *   2025-11-13 afm-filmindustry-a ........ AI & commercial content
 *   2025-11-13 afm-filmindustry-b ........ Level Up: filmmaking & gaming
 *   2025-11-15 afm-western-alliance-bank . the client reception
 *   2025-11-16 afm-pitch-conference ...... the AFM Pitch Conference
 *   2025-11-16 afm-independent-film ...... FLIP Creative's indie-action panel
 *
 * PHOTO↔SESSION MAPPING (the basis for every pairing below): the AFM Sessions
 * backdrop never names the session, so frames are matched to dispatches by
 * capture timestamp from the source filenames (see scripts/make-venture-story
 * .mjs + make-linkedin-archive-additions.mjs) cross-referenced against panel
 * head-count. Nov 12 09:37/09:38 (5 panelists, sponsor lectern) → the Wednesday
 * morning finance session; 11:37 → the second Wednesday session; Nov 13 11:07
 * (5 panelists) → the AI session; 13:08 (4 panelists, matching the four named
 * in his post) → Level Up; Nov 14 18:30/18:34 → the Western Alliance reception;
 * Nov 15 09:57–10:20 (a solo presenter at the sponsor podium, three seated
 * listeners) → the Pitch Conference; Nov 15 16:04/16:09 (6 panelists, matching
 * the six named) → the FLIP closing panel. Because that mapping is inference
 * rather than in-frame proof, CAPTIONS state only day + what is visible; the
 * PROSE carries the session names, which come from his own posts.
 *
 * CROP NOTE: every bake here is NATIVELY SQUARE (1:1), so each beat crops via
 * CSS aspect-ratio + object-position, tuned against rendered crop previews
 * (eye-reviewed, not blind). Three crops from the previous build were plain
 * wrong and are fixed: the old hero cropped 16:9 @60% put a dead ceiling band
 * over panelists cut off at the knees; podium-speaker at 4:5 @60% cut the
 * speaker off at the neck; the closing reception at 1:1 was three-quarters
 * marquee ceiling. Originals untouched on disk; no new bakes.
 *
 * FIXED FACTUAL ERROR: the previous build's Movement 3 diptych captioned
 * panel-independent-action + panel-address-stage "The indie-action-films panel,
 * two angles." They are not the same panel — six hours apart, six panelists vs
 * three. The genuine two-angle pair is the old `hero` frame + panel-independent
 * -action (16:04 and 16:09, same six panelists), and that is what the closing
 * movement now runs; panel-address-stage moves to the Pitch Conference, where
 * its solo-presenter-plus-three-listeners composition actually belongs.
 *
 * Image budget: 19 of the 28 bakes render. NOT RENDERED (no-silent-caps rule):
 *   evening-reception-disco ... operator-excluded (unbranded nightclub frame,
 *                               the class of shot he has twice rejected)
 *   market-floor-lounge ....... redundant with pavilion-row-egypt's empty-floor
 *   veritone-booth ............ a flat vendor banner; reads as an ad, not a room
 *   podium-speaker ............ same presenter, same minute as podium-lectern
 *   podium-address is kept .... (a different presenter — deliberately kept)
 *   reception-tent-toast ...... too dark to hold at any scale
 *   step-and-repeat, step-and-repeat-2 ... the evening red carpet is a different
 *                               event's backdrop (Hollywood Film & TV Motel)
 *   venue-signage ............. an Arizona Film & Digital Media lounge, off-theme
 *   panel-seated is kept ...... (used for the AI session)
 *
 * HONESTY: the only status claim is the AFM LegendMark (tier `attended`),
 * rendered near the top from consts — verbatim, never upgraded. The prose is
 * condensed from the operator's OWN posts; named people appear only as he named
 * them, and no relationship beyond attendance is claimed.
 */
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { PAGES } from "../../consts";
import { existsSync } from "node:fs";
import { join } from "node:path";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const STORY = "afm-2025";
const mediaDir = join(process.cwd(), "public", "media", "venture", STORY);

async function shot(slug: string, alt: string) {
  const webp900 = join(mediaDir, `${slug}-900.webp`);
  if (!existsSync(webp900)) return undefined;
  const url = (w: number, ext: string) => `${base}/media/venture/${STORY}/${slug}-${w}.${ext}`;
  return {
    alt,
    avif: `${url(900, "avif")} 900w, ${url(1600, "avif")} 1600w`,
    webp: `${url(900, "webp")} 900w, ${url(1600, "webp")} 1600w`,
    fallback: url(900, "webp"),
  };
}

// ── Movement 1 · arrival ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
const venueCenturyPlaza = await shot("venue-century-plaza", "The Fairmont Century Plaza hotel exterior at night, lit facade and a large woven-wire sculpture out front.");

// ── Movement 2 · the floor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
const pavilionRowEgypt = await shot("pavilion-row-egypt", "A row of exhibitor booths at American Film Market, including a banner for the Egypt Film Commission promoting local production incentives.");
const pavilionCinecitta = await shot("pavilion-cinecitta", "A trade-show banner for Cinecittà studios in Rome, advertising soundstages and an Italian production tax incentive, at an AFM exhibitor pavilion.");
const wrapbookPillar = await shot("wrapbook-pillar", "A hotel lobby column wrapped in Wrapbook branding reading “the AI platform for paying crew and vendors.”");
const floorWvfilmBooth = await shot("floor-wvfilm-booth", "Two attendees talking with representatives at the West Virginia Film Office booth, browsing location photos and brochures at their market table.");

// ── Movement 3 · the finance sessions (Wed) ────────────────────────────────
const heroFinance = await shot("panel-finance-packaging", "Five panelists seated on the AFM Sessions stage before the American Film Market backdrop, one speaking into a handheld microphone.");
const stageBeforeDoors = await shot("stage-before-doors", "An empty conference stage under blue lighting, American Film Market branding on the backdrop, five white chairs set out and a side screen showing session Q&A instructions.");
const panelMics = await shot("panel-mics", "The same stage from further back — a moderator at a sponsor lectern on the left, five panelists seated under the American Film Market backdrop.");
const panelStage = await shot("panel-stage", "Four panelists on the AFM Sessions stage, one mid-answer with a microphone, the AFM Sessions red circle mark behind them.");

// ── Movement 4 · AI and games (Thu) ────────────────────────────────────────
const panelSeated = await shot("panel-seated", "A moderator and five panelists seated across the AFM Sessions stage, notes in hand.");
const panelLevelUp = await shot("panel-levelup-gaming", "Four panelists seated on the AFM Sessions stage, the moderator on the left mid-question.");

// ── Movement 5 · after hours (Fri) ─────────────────────────────────────────
const westernAlliancePodium = await shot("western-alliance-podium", "A line of hosts and guests on a small stage under a Western Alliance Bank backdrop, one speaking from a Maybourne Beverly Hills lectern.");
const westernAllianceLineup = await shot("western-alliance-lineup", "The same reception from the back of the room, guests watching the Western Alliance Bank stage over a crowd of heads.");
const receptionEvening = await shot("reception-evening", "Guests mingling at an evening marquee reception under chandeliers, a step-and-repeat backdrop visible in the background.");

// ── Movement 6 · the Pitch Conference (Sat morning) ────────────────────────
const podiumAddress = await shot("podium-address", "A presenter in a red jumpsuit speaking at a standing microphone beside the sponsor podium, her image mirrored on the big screen behind her.");
const panelAddressStage = await shot("panel-address-stage", "A presenter standing at a microphone on the left of the AFM Sessions stage, three listeners seated in chairs to his right.");
const podiumLectern = await shot("podium-lectern", "The same presenter mid-gesture beside the sponsor lectern, his face filling the big screen behind him.");

// ── Movement 7 · the closing panel (Sat afternoon) ─────────────────────────
const flipPanelWide = await shot("hero", "Six panelists seated across the AFM Sessions stage before the American Film Market backdrop.");
const flipPanelClose = await shot("panel-independent-action", "The same six panelists from closer in, a caption slide visible on the side screen.");

// The AFM proof line — imported from consts, rendered VERBATIM. This is the only
// status claim on the page (tier `attended`), and it sits up top.
const afmRoom = PAGES.entertainment.rooms.items.find(
  (r) => r.proof.relationshipId === "afm",
);

/*
  ⚠ OPERATOR READ-APPROVAL REQUIRED — the movement kickers, both pull quotes,
  and every caption below are visible copy (staging-only until read). The prose
  is condensed from his own nine AFM posts; the two questions quoted are the
  ones he published asking. Caption facts trace only to what is verifiable
  in-frame (Cinecittà / Egypt Film Commission / West Virginia Film Office /
  Wrapbook / Western Alliance Bank signage) or to the day of capture.
*/
const KICKERS = {
  m1: "Monday · Arrival",
  m2: "Tuesday · The Floor",
  m3: "Wednesday · Where The Money Sits",
  m4: "Thursday · Machines And Worlds",
  m5: "Friday · After Hours",
  m6: "Saturday morning · The Pitch Conference",
  m7: "Saturday afternoon · The Last Panel",
};
const PULL_A = "Distribution starts at the deal table. The earlier it's integrated, the more leverage you keep.";
const PULL_B = "Use AI to buy back time and optionality — not to outsource taste and responsibility.";
const PULL_C = "The story tells us why we care. The strategy tells us why it works.";

const CAPTIONS = {
  hero: "Wednesday morning on the AFM Sessions stage.",
  venueCenturyPlaza: "The Fairmont Century Plaza at night — the market fills the building for a week.",
  pavilionRowEgypt: "The pavilion rows, mid-morning — the Egypt Film Commission a few aisles in.",
  pavilionDiptych: "The floor sells itself: Cinecittà's soundstages, Wrapbook's crew-payment pitch.",
  wvfilmBooth: "Booth-level trade at the West Virginia Film Office table.",
  stageBeforeDoors: "Before doors, Wednesday morning — five chairs set out, the Q&A instructions already up.",
  panelMics: "The same session as the frame at the top of this page, from further back — the sponsor lectern in view.",
  panelStage: "Later the same morning, a second session on the same stage.",
  panelSeated: "Thursday, late morning — six people and one question about what stays human.",
  panelLevelUp: "Thursday afternoon: four panelists, two industries.",
  westernAllianceDiptych: "Friday evening at the Western Alliance Bank reception — from the stage, and from the back of the room.",
  receptionEvening: "The market moves outside — an evening reception under the marquee.",
  pitchPair: "Saturday morning at the Pitch Conference: two minutes, one presenter at a time.",
  podiumLectern: "The screen carries whoever has the floor.",
  flipDiptych: "The closing panel, two angles, five minutes apart.",
};

const meta = {
  title: "American Film Market 2025 — a venture story",
  description:
    "American Film Market 2025 in Los Angeles, California — a week of AFM Sessions panels on finance, AI, gaming and independent film, the Pitch Conference, and the market floor itself.",
};
---

<Base title={meta.title} description={meta.description}>
  <main class="vs" data-lane="venture" style="--ground:#10141f">
    <section class="vs-head">
      <a href={`${base}/venture`} class="link vs-back"><span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">←</span> Venture</a>

      <p class="hud-label vs-slate" data-reveal>American Film Market · Nov 8–15, 2025 · Los Angeles, CA</p>

      <h1 class="vs-headline" data-reveal>
        <span class="vs-line">AFM</span>
        <span class="vs-line vs-line--push"><span class="vs-turn">Los Angeles,</span> 2025</span>
      </h1>

      {afmRoom && (
        <div class="vs-proof" data-reveal>
        </div>
      )}
    </section>

    {heroFinance && (
      <figure class="vs-hero" data-reveal>
        <picture>
          <source type="image/avif" srcset={heroFinance.avif} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 78rem" />
          <source type="image/webp" srcset={heroFinance.webp} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 78rem" />
          <img src={heroFinance.fallback} alt={heroFinance.alt} style="aspect-ratio:16/9;object-fit:cover;object-position:50% 62%" loading="eager" decoding="async" />
        </picture>
        <figcaption class="vs-cap">{CAPTIONS.hero}</figcaption>
      </figure>
    )}

    <!-- ══ MOVEMENT 1 · ARRIVAL ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <section class="vs-movement" data-reveal>
      <p class="hud-label vs-kicker">{KICKERS.m1}</p>
      <div class="vs-prose">
        <p>
          Kicking off the week at the American Film Market was energizing and
          grounding at once — a place where creativity feels tangible and every
          conversation holds a spark of possibility. Even getting around Los Angeles
          was an adventure: a first ride in a Waymo, one of those quietly
          futuristic moments — a reminder of how fast storytelling and technology
          are converging.
        </p>
        <p>
          The connections we were most excited to explore were around drone
          cinematography and story-driven brand strategy — helping ideas find both
          their visual and emotional altitude.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {venueCenturyPlaza && (
      <figure class="vs-widest" data-reveal>
        <picture>
          <source type="image/avif" srcset={venueCenturyPlaza.avif} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 86rem" />
          <source type="image/webp" srcset={venueCenturyPlaza.webp} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 86rem" />
          <img src={venueCenturyPlaza.fallback} alt={venueCenturyPlaza.alt} style="aspect-ratio:2/1;object-fit:cover;object-position:50% 68%" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </picture>
        <figcaption class="vs-cap">{CAPTIONS.venueCenturyPlaza}</figcaption>
      </figure>
    )}

    <!-- ══ MOVEMENT 2 · THE FLOOR ════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <section class="vs-movement" data-reveal>
      <p class="hud-label vs-kicker">{KICKERS.m2}</p>
      <h2 class="vs-floor-heading">The rest of the building.</h2>
      <div class="vs-prose">
        <p>
          The AFM Sessions panels get the spotlight and the recap posts. The market
          itself is what actually fills the building — a trading floor for film and
          television, built for a week inside the Fairmont Century Plaza in Century
          City.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="vs-duo" data-reveal>
      {pavilionRowEgypt && (
        <figure class="vs-duo-fig">
          <picture>
            <source type="image/avif" srcset={pavilionRowEgypt.avif} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 30rem" />
            <source type="image/webp" srcset={pavilionRowEgypt.webp} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 30rem" />
            <img src={pavilionRowEgypt.fallback} alt={pavilionRowEgypt.alt} style="aspect-ratio:16/9;object-fit:cover;object-position:50% 50%" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </picture>
          <figcaption class="vs-cap">{CAPTIONS.pavilionRowEgypt}</figcaption>
        </figure>
      )}
      <div class="vs-prose vs-prose--inrow">
        <p>
          Walk past the stage and the floor opens into rows of exhibitor pavilions:
          national film commissions and studio groups, each with a booth, a banner,
          and a pitch — Cinecittà promoting Rome's soundstages a few aisles from the
          Egypt Film Commission's rebate pitch, and the vendors who keep a
          production running wrapped around the lobby columns in between.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    {(pavilionCinecitta || wrapbookPillar) && (
      <figure class="vs-diptych" data-reveal>
        <div class="vs-diptych-row">
          {pavilionCinecitta && (
            <picture>
              <source type="image/avif" srcset={pavilionCinecitta.avif} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 50vw, 26rem" />
              <source type="image/webp" srcset={pavilionCinecitta.webp} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 50vw, 26rem" />
              <img src={pavilionCinecitta.fallback} alt={pavilionCinecitta.alt} style="aspect-ratio:4/5;object-fit:cover;object-position:50% 45%" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            </picture>
          )}
          {wrapbookPillar && (
            <picture>
              <source type="image/avif" srcset={wrapbookPillar.avif} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 50vw, 26rem" />
              <source type="image/webp" srcset={wrapbookPillar.webp} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 50vw, 26rem" />
              <img src={wrapbookPillar.fallback} alt={wrapbookPillar.alt} style="aspect-ratio:4/5;object-fit:cover;object-position:50% 40%" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            </picture>
          )}
        </div>
        <figcaption class="vs-cap">{CAPTIONS.pavilionDiptych}</figcaption>
      </figure>
    )}

    <div class="vs-duo vs-duo--flip" data-reveal>
      <div class="vs-prose vs-prose--inrow">
        <p>
          The actual work happens at booth level, in short conversations that repeat
          all week — a state or regional film office's table, a producer stopping to
          ask about incentives, locations, crews. It's unglamorous compared to a
          stage moment, and it's also the real transaction the whole event exists to
          enable.
        </p>
      </div>
      {floorWvfilmBooth && (
        <figure class="vs-duo-fig">
          <picture>
            <source type="image/avif" srcset={floorWvfilmBooth.avif} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 34rem" />
            <source type="image/webp" srcset={floorWvfilmBooth.webp} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 34rem" />
            <img src={floorWvfilmBooth.fallback} alt={floorWvfilmBooth.alt} style="aspect-ratio:4/3;object-fit:cover;object-position:50% 55%" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </picture>
          <figcaption class="vs-cap">{CAPTIONS.wvfilmBooth}</figcaption>
        </figure>
      )}
    </div>

    <!-- ══ MOVEMENT 3 · WEDNESDAY, THE FINANCE SESSIONS ══════════════════ -->
    <section class="vs-movement" data-reveal>
      <p class="hud-label vs-kicker">{KICKERS.m3}</p>
      <div class="vs-prose">
        <p>
          A recurring theme all week, from filmmakers and producers alike, was
          distribution. So many creatives had poured everything into something
          genuinely remarkable — secured the funding, finished the film — only to
          face the uphill climb of finding fair and effective distribution. It's a
          reminder of how much the business behind the art matters.
        </p>
        <p>
          So at <em>Finance I: From Packaging to Payback</em> — Jeaneane Davey, Sam
          Pressman, Josh Rosenbaum, Jeff Caruso and Jeremy Ross — that's the question
          we put to the panel from the floor: how early should distribution strategy
          be built into financing to reduce risk and improve outcomes for filmmakers?
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {stageBeforeDoors && (
      <figure class="vs-fullbeat" data-reveal>
        <picture>
          <source type="image/avif" srcset={stageBeforeDoors.avif} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 60rem" />
          <source type="image/webp" srcset={stageBeforeDoors.webp} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 60rem" />
          <img src={stageBeforeDoors.fallback} alt={stageBeforeDoors.alt} style="aspect-ratio:16/9;object-fit:cover;object-position:50% 72%" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </picture>
        <figcaption class="vs-cap">{CAPTIONS.stageBeforeDoors}</figcaption>
      </figure>
    )}

    <div class="vs-duo" data-reveal>
      {panelMics && (
        <figure class="vs-duo-fig">
          <picture>
            <source type="image/avif" srcset={panelMics.avif} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 30rem" />
            <source type="image/webp" srcset={panelMics.webp} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 30rem" />
            <img src={panelMics.fallback} alt={panelMics.alt} style="aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover;object-position:50% 62%" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </picture>
          <figcaption class="vs-cap">{CAPTIONS.panelMics}</figcaption>
        </figure>
      )}
      <div class="vs-prose vs-prose--inrow">
        <p>
          The answers were practical. Align production size with audience and
          release scale. Treat incentives as multi-dimensional — real value comes
          from local partners, crew infrastructure and monetization speed, not just
          a rebate percentage. And transparency in reporting and recoupment terms is
          still overdue across the whole financing chain.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <blockquote class="vs-pull" data-reveal>{PULL_A}</blockquote>

    <section class="vs-movement" data-reveal>
      <div class="vs-prose">
        <p>
          Later the same morning, <em>The Innovators: From Fan to Financier</em> —
          Willie Morris of Waterfall, Marc Iserlis of Republic Film, and Christopher
          Woodrow of Media Capital Technologies — took the other half of the
          equation. Crowd investing isn't crowdfunding: retail investors can now
          take real stakes, which turns fans into early financiers and day-one
          advocates. Community becomes capital. Emerging models are experimenting
          with equity participation for cast and below-the-line, and new entrants
          are aiming straight at the mid-budget theatrical gap studio consolidation
          left behind.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {panelStage && (
      <figure class="vs-fullbeat" data-reveal>
        <picture>
          <source type="image/avif" srcset={panelStage.avif} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 60rem" />
          <source type="image/webp" srcset={panelStage.webp} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 60rem" />
          <img src={panelStage.fallback} alt={panelStage.alt} style="aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover;object-position:50% 72%" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </picture>
        <figcaption class="vs-cap">{CAPTIONS.panelStage}</figcaption>
      </figure>
    )}

    <!-- ══ MOVEMENT 4 · THURSDAY, AI AND GAMES ═══════════════════════════ -->
    <section class="vs-movement" data-reveal>
      <p class="hud-label vs-kicker">{KICKERS.m4}</p>
      <div class="vs-prose">
        <p>
          <em>Visualizing the Future of Commercial Content: What AI Means for the
          Industry</em> — Scott Martin, Darren Frankel, Lori McCreary, Ted Schilowitz
          and Nelson Granados. Living at the crossroads of storytelling, commercial
          content and emerging tech, our question was the practical one: which parts
          of the workflow are already proving the highest ROI with AI, and which
          should stay human-driven no matter what ships next?
        </p>
        <p>
          The answers circled a few themes. AI is already strong at assistive work —
          cleanup, localization, asset management, crowd replacement, the variations
          that used to eat days of budget. Story and performance still need
          authentic intelligence; tools help, but the emotional choices come from
          people. The real fault line isn't AI versus no AI, it's ethical versus
          unregulated AI — training data, IP ownership and consent decide whether an
          industry commoditizes the very creators it's built on. And if AI absorbs
          all the entry-level work, where do the next editors, cinematographers and
          producers learn the craft?
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    {panelSeated && (
      <figure class="vs-fullbeat" data-reveal>
        <picture>
          <source type="image/avif" srcset={panelSeated.avif} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 60rem" />
          <source type="image/webp" srcset={panelSeated.webp} sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 60rem" />
          <img src={panelSeated.fallback} alt={panelSeated.alt} style="aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover;object-position:50% 60%" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </picture>
        <figcaption class="vs-cap">{CAPTIONS.panelSeated}</figcaption>
      </figure>
    )}

    <blockquote class="vs-pull" data-reveal>{PULL_B}</blockquote>

    <div class="vs-duo vs-duo--flip" data-reveal>
      <div class="vs-prose vs-prose--inrow">
        <p>
          That afternoon, <em>Level Up: The Evolving Relationship Between Filmmaking
          and Gaming</em> — Blake Rochkind, Russell Binder, Patrick Sweeney and
          Amanda Kruse. What landed wasn't the momentum around games becoming films,
          but how different the creative logic of the two mediums really is. Indie
          games are becoming IP engines, with fandoms often larger than the books and
          comics that routinely get adapted. Modern engines mean more worlds, not
          fewer. And transmedia works when you make one great thing first — audiences
          can feel the difference between a living world and a cash grab.
        </p>
      </div>
      {panelLevelUp && (
        <figure class="vs-duo-fig">
          <picture>
            <source type="image/avif" srcset={panelLevelUp.avif} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 34rem" />
            <source type="image/webp" srcset={panelLevelUp.webp} sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 34rem" />
            <img src={panelLevelUp.fallback} alt={panelLevelUp.alt} style="aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover;object-position:50% 70%" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </picture>
          <figcaption class="vs-cap">{CAPTIONS.panelLevelUp}</figcaption>
        </figure>
      )}
    </div>

